## 2. Problem Space

### 2.1 The Broken Status Quo

Here's how a typical 200-person company without procurement currently buys:

```
Employee needs a tool
    → Asks manager on Slack
        → Manager says "ask finance"
            → Finance says "send me an email"
                → Email sits for 3 days
                    → CFO asks "do we already have something like this?"
                        → Nobody knows
                            → CFO approves via reply-all
                                → Employee signs up with corporate card
                                    → Invoice arrives 30 days later
                                        → Finance has no idea what it's for
                                            → Reconciliation nightmare
```

**Average time from "I need this" to "I have this":** 11.4 days for a $500/month SaaS tool.

**Percentage of purchases that follow any formal process:** <20% in companies without procurement.

### 2.2 The Cost of No Process

| Impact Area | Metric | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Maverick spend (off-policy purchases) | 40-60% of all indirect spend | Hackett Group 2025 |
| Duplicate subscriptions | 29% of SaaS tools have functional overlap | Zylo 2025 SaaS Report |
| Budget overruns from untracked spend | 23% average overspend | Gartner Procurement Research |
| Time wasted on manual procurement tasks | 8.3 hours/week per finance team member | APQC Benchmarking |
| Invoice processing cost (manual) | $15-40 per invoice | Ardent Partners |
| Invoice processing cost (automated) | $2-5 per invoice | Ardent Partners |

### 2.3 Why Existing Solutions Fail SMBs

**Enterprise tools (Coupa, SAP Ariba, GEP):**
- 6-12 month implementation
- $100K+ annual cost
- Require dedicated procurement staff to operate
- Designed for complexity, not simplicity

**SaaS management tools (Freqens, Spendflo, Vendr):**
- Only cover renewals/renegotiation
- No intake, no approval workflows, no PO management
- No budget enforcement at point of purchase
- Passive (alert-based) not active (execution-based)

**Card-based spend tools (Ramp, Brex, Spendesk):**
- Payment-centric, not process-centric
- Limited pre-purchase controls
- No structured intake for non-card purchases
- Weak on services/contract procurement

**The gap:** Nobody owns the full intake-to-pay lifecycle for the 50-500 employee company with zero procurement headcount. That's Reqflow.

