## 3. Competitive Teardown: Why Freqens Isn't Enough

### 3.1 What Freqens Does Well

Credit where due - Freqens nails these:

- **Renewal detection:** AI reads contracts, extracts dates, alerts the right person at the right time. Genuinely solves the "we forgot to cancel and auto-renewed at a 20% markup" problem.
- **Price benchmarking:** Anonymized data from 500+ companies gives you leverage. You know if you're paying above market.
- **Vendor alternatives:** One-click competitor discovery with real pricing data. Saves hours of manual research.
- **Team satisfaction surveys:** Quantifies whether the team even wants to keep a tool before you negotiate.
- **Speed to value:** Upload contracts, get insights in hours. No implementation project.

### 3.2 The 14 Critical Gaps Freqens Leaves Wide Open

#### Gap 1: No Purchase Intake / Request Management
**What's missing:** There is no way for an employee to say "I need to buy something" inside Freqens. No intake form. No request submission. No structured way to capture what's needed, why, and by when.

**User impact:** Requests continue happening via Slack, email, hallway conversations. No single source of truth. No audit trail. The CFO doesn't know what's being requested until money has already been spent.

**Why it matters:** Intake is the #1 driver of procurement compliance. Without a front door, every purchase is maverick spend by definition.

#### Gap 2: No Approval Workflows
**What's missing:** No routing engine. No multi-level approval chains. No conditional logic (e.g., "over $5K needs VP + Finance sign-off"). No delegation when someone's on vacation.

**User impact:** Approvals happen ad-hoc via email/Slack. No visibility into who approved what and when. No escalation when approvals stall. Budget owners can't enforce policy because there's no policy engine.

**Why it matters:** Without approval workflows, there's no governance. Finance discovers spend after the fact, not before. This is the opposite of spend control.

#### Gap 3: No Budget Tracking or Enforcement
**What's missing:** No department budgets. No project budgets. No real-time budget consumption tracking. No alerts when a budget is 80% consumed. No hard stops when a budget is exceeded.

**User impact:** The CFO has a spreadsheet with annual budget allocations. Nobody checks it before buying. Departments overspend consistently. Budget reviews happen monthly at best, revealing problems weeks too late.

**Why it matters:** Budget enforcement at the point of purchase is the single highest-ROI procurement control. Every dollar saved here is pure margin.

#### Gap 4: No Purchase Order (PO) Creation or Management
**What's missing:** Cannot create, issue, send, or track purchase orders. No PO numbers. No 3-way matching (PO vs. receipt vs. invoice).

**User impact:** Vendors receive informal emails or nothing at all. There's no contractual commitment record. When invoices arrive, finance can't match them to authorized purchases. Disputes are impossible to resolve without a PO trail.

**Why it matters:** POs are the backbone of procurement compliance and AP efficiency. Without them, every invoice is a surprise.

#### Gap 5: No Invoice Processing
**What's missing:** No invoice capture (email, portal, or OCR). No matching. No approval routing for invoices. No payment tracking. No AP automation.

**User impact:** Finance manually enters every invoice. Duplicates slip through. Early payment discounts are missed because invoices sit in someone's inbox. Month-end close is a firefight.

**Why it matters:** AP automation delivers 60-80% cost reduction per invoice. For a company processing 200+ invoices/month, this is tens of thousands in savings annually.

#### Gap 6: No Vendor Onboarding or Compliance
**What's missing:** No vendor registration portal. No document collection (W-9, insurance, certifications). No risk assessment. No due diligence workflow.

**User impact:** Vendors are added to the system (if there even is one) manually by finance. Compliance documents live in random folders. When audit time comes, nobody can produce the required vendor documentation.

**Why it matters:** Vendor compliance is a regulatory requirement in many industries. Without it, you're exposed to legal, financial, and operational risk.

#### Gap 7: No Spend Policy Engine
**What's missing:** No configurable rules like "no SaaS purchase over $1K without security review," "all professional services need 2 quotes," or "marketing can't buy tools already covered by existing licenses."

**User impact:** Every purchase decision is ad-hoc. The same mistakes (buying duplicates, skipping security review, ignoring preferred vendors) happen repeatedly because there's no systematic way to enforce rules.

**Why it matters:** Policies without enforcement are just suggestions. A policy engine makes governance automatic, not aspirational.

#### Gap 8: Only Addresses Renewals, Not New Purchases
**What's missing:** Freqens activates when a renewal date approaches. But 40-60% of indirect spend comes from NEW purchases - first-time tools, new service contracts, one-time purchases. Freqens has nothing for these.

**User impact:** The first purchase of a tool has zero process. No benchmarking (Freqens needs an existing contract to benchmark against). No negotiation prep. No alternatives analysis. The employee picks whatever they found on Google and signs up.

**Why it matters:** The first purchase sets the baseline. Overpaying by 20% on the first contract means you're negotiating down from an inflated starting point for every renewal after.

#### Gap 9: No Spend Visibility Dashboard (Real-Time)
**What's missing:** Freqens shows contract-level data. It does not show real-time spend by department, category, vendor, or project. No burn-rate tracking. No forecasting.

**User impact:** The CFO can see "we have a Salesforce contract for $50K/year" but can't see "engineering has consumed 78% of their Q1 software budget with 6 weeks left in the quarter."

**Why it matters:** Real-time spend visibility is the difference between reactive finance (discovering problems at month-end) and proactive finance (preventing problems before they happen).

#### Gap 10: No Contract Lifecycle Management
**What's missing:** Freqens monitors contract dates. But it doesn't manage contracts: no versioning, no clause tracking, no amendment management, no obligation tracking, no contract creation from templates.

**User impact:** Contracts live in email attachments, Google Drive, or the vendor's portal. Nobody can find the latest version. Obligation tracking ("we committed to a minimum volume") is nonexistent.

**Why it matters:** Contract management is a governance requirement. Without it, you don't actually know what you've agreed to.

#### Gap 11: No Multi-Stakeholder Collaboration
**What's missing:** No shared workspace for purchase decisions. No commenting. No @mentions. No decision threads. No voting or consensus mechanism.

**User impact:** Purchase decisions involve 3-5 people (requester, manager, IT, security, finance) but the conversation happens across 4 different channels with no central record.

**Why it matters:** The #1 reason procurement processes break down is coordination failure. Multi-stakeholder collaboration isn't a nice-to-have - it's the core workflow.

#### Gap 12: No ERP/Accounting Integration for Transactions
**What's missing:** Freqens doesn't push data into accounting systems. No journal entry creation. No cost center allocation. No accrual management.

**User impact:** Finance manually re-enters data from Freqens into their accounting system. Double work. Risk of transcription errors. Reconciliation is manual.

**Why it matters:** Integration eliminates the "last mile" problem. Without it, the tool creates work instead of eliminating it.

#### Gap 13: No Catalog / Preferred Vendor Management
**What's missing:** No way to define "these are our approved vendors for cloud hosting" or "use these preferred consultancies." No catalog where employees can browse pre-negotiated options.

**User impact:** Employees Google for solutions every time. They don't know about existing contracts with volume discounts. Spend is fragmented across dozens of vendors instead of consolidated with preferred partners.

**Why it matters:** Preferred vendor programs typically save 15-25% through volume consolidation. Without a catalog, every purchase starts from zero.

#### Gap 14: No Mobile Experience for Approvals
**What's missing:** No mobile app. No push notifications for pending approvals. No quick-approve from phone.

**User impact:** Approvals (in whatever ad-hoc form they take) stall when the approver is traveling, in meetings, or away from their laptop. Average approval delay: 2.3 days.

**Why it matters:** Speed-to-approval directly impacts employee satisfaction and vendor relationships. A VP who can approve a $2K request from their phone in 30 seconds vs. needing to log into email and write a response is a 10x improvement.

